{
  "scenario_id": "sc-042304",
  "overall_assessment": "anomalies",
  "findings": [
    {
      "benefit": "CI",
      "years": [2021, 2022],
      "pattern": "shock",
      "direction": "increase",
      "magnitude": "A/E ~1.27-1.30 for two flat-topped years vs ~0.92-0.94 baseline (net +0.36 over the window); 2021: 1764 vs 1386 expected (+27%), 2022: 2005 vs 1548 (+30%); single-year jump +0.39 in 2020->2021, full reversion to ~0.94 in 2023-2024",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "evidence": [
        "ae_ci_by_year.csv: 2020 A/E 0.880 -> 2021 1.273 -> 2022 1.295 -> 2023 0.937 -> 2024 0.944; level arrives in one year, holds flat (flat-topped 2y), no gradual build-up (max 1yr move 0.393 vs net excursion +0.362, onset ratio 1.09)",
        "Evidence pack CI: excursion window 2021-2022 +0.362 (score 20.1), step +0.196 at 2021; per-year z +10.2/+11.6 treated as indicative only because x6.33 YoY overdispersion is itself driven by this excursion, so no separate Poisson-year flagging",
        "Context: CI ran 0.84-0.95 (pooled ~0.92) in 2015-2020 and settled back to ~0.94 in 2023-2024, i.e. the spike is transient, not a permanent new level; cross-line block shows no coordinated multi-line move in 2021-2022 (only 1 line >0.08 from its own baseline per year), so this is a CI-specific event"
      ],
      "recommended_action": "Pull 2021-2022 CI claim files and review: diagnosis mix shift (e.g. cancer/cardiovascular share), severity lumpiness and large-policyholder concentration, coding/adjudication or benefit-definition changes effective in 2021, and late-reported/IBNR build effects. Since A/E fully reverted to ~0.94 in 2023-2024, treat as a transient spike unless driver review identifies a recurring cause; verify 2023-2024 experience is stable before concluding no lasting change to CI incidence/severity pricing or valuation assumptions."
    }
  ]
}

